{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>And Nebo, and Baal Meon, whose names were changed</b> – Nevo and Baal Meon are names of idolatry, for the Emorites would name their cities after their idols, and the children of Reuven transformed their names into other names. This is the meaning of 'מוּסַבֹּת שֵׁם' (whose names were changed) — Nevo and Baal Meon were transformed to another name; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 32:38). But the verse that says, 'And they called by names the names of the cities which they built' — this refers to all of them, not to Nevo and Baal Meon alone. Furthermore, what reason would there be for Scripture to mention them by the name of the idolatry and say that the children of Reuven built them and changed their names, yet not mention the good name substituted for them? For the customary practice of Scripture regarding captured cities is always to mention the new name: 'And they called Leshem Dan, after the name of Dan their father' (Joshua 19:47); 'And he called them Havot Yair' (Numbers 32:41); 'And he called it Novach' (Numbers 32:42). Likewise in all cases of transformed names Scripture mentions them: 'And the name of Hebron previously was Kiryat Arba' (Joshua 14:15); 'And the name of Devir previously was Kiryat Sefer' (ibid. 15:15). And what appears correct to me is that these names were the names of the cities when they were in the possession of Moav, and Sihon conquered all these cities from the first king of Moav, for thus it is written regarding Heshbon and Dibon and Medeva, as it says, 'For fire went out from Heshbon' etc. (Numbers 21:30). And so it would seem to me from the words of Yiftach, who said: 'While Israel dwelt in Heshbon and its towns, and in Aroer and its towns, and in all the cities that are along the Arnon' (Judges 11:26) — and that is Aroer which they took from the hand of Sihon. Now behold, when Tiglat Pileser king of Assyria exiled the Reuveni and the Gadi, Moav returned and settled in their land and in their cities, for we find in the book of Yirmeyahu (chapter 48) that when Nevuchadnetzar devastated Moav, he captured from them all these cities, and he mentioned by name Heshbon and Elale and Kiryatayim and Dibon and Nevo and Yazir and Aroer and Baal Meon, which he called Beit Meon, and Beit Nimrah, which he called Nimrim, and Yachtzah — and behold they are all for Moav. And likewise in Yeshayahu (chapters 15–16), when Sancheriv swept over Moav, most of these cities were mentioned there, for he exiled the Reuveni and the Gadi first, and afterward swept over Ammon and Moav. Therefore it appears that the meaning of <b>מוּסַבֹּת שֵׁם</b> is that all these cities are 'מוּסַבֹּת שֵׁם,' for the Emorite had changed their original name when he captured them, and this had been their original name when they were in the hand of Moav, and the children of Reuven and Gad further called them by names when they rebuilt them. And Scripture did not say 'and they called names to the cities which they built,' but rather said 'and they called by names the names of the cities,' for they called them by the original names that they had from the first, for they wished to refer to them by the name that had been known to them of old, or to shame Moav, or as our Sages mentioned, that the Emorites would name their cities after their idolatry — but we have not seen in Yehoshua that he changed the names of the cities that he conquered." "And the meaning of <b>and Sibmah</b> — that they built Sibmah, and it was not one whose name was changed."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ע״ז stands for עבודה זרה (idolatry/foreign worship); resolved by context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:20.603974135Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}